      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\), we will use the properties of logarithms and exponents. Here are the steps to solve it:\n\n1. **Rewrite the square root as an exponent:**\n   \\[\n   27\\sqrt{3} = 27 \\cdot 3^{1/2}\n   \\]\n   So, the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 (27 \\cdot 3^{1/2})\n   \\]\n\n2. **Combine the exponents using the product rule of logarithms:**\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 (27 \\cdot 3^{1/2}) = \\log_3 27 + \\log_3 (3^{1/2})\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify \\(\\log_3 27\\) and \\(\\log_3 (3^{1/2})\\):**\n   - For \\(\\log_3 27\\), note that \\(27 = 3^3\\):\n     \\[\n     \\log_3 27 = \\log_3 (3^3) = 3\n     \\]\n   - For \\(\\log_3 (3^{1/2})\\):\n     \\[\n     \\log_3 (3^{1/2}) = \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Add the simplified logarithms:**\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 27 + \\log_3 (3^{1/2}) = 3 + \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine the terms to form an improper fraction:**\n   \\[\n   3 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
